.TH ENVDIR 1 "March 2026" "envdir" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
envdir - run a command with environment variables from a directory

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B envdir
.I DIR
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS... ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B envdir
reads environment variables from files in
.I DIR
and runs
.I COMMAND
with those variables applied to its environment.

Each file in
.I DIR
represents one environment variable.

The filename becomes the variable name.

The first line of the file becomes the variable value.

Empty files cause the variable to be removed from the environment.

This behavior is inspired by the
.B envdir
tool from the
.B daemontools
suite by D. J. Bernstein.

.SH DIRECTORY FORMAT

Each regular file in the directory represents one environment variable.

Example directory:

.nf
env/
DB_HOST
DB_PORT
DEBUG
.fi

.SH FILE NAME

The filename becomes the environment variable name.

Example:

.nf
env/DB_HOST
.fi

Creates:

.nf
DB_HOST=value
.fi

.SH FILE CONTENTS

The first line of the file becomes the variable value.

Trailing spaces and tabs are removed.

NUL bytes are converted to newline characters.

Example:

.nf
env/API_KEY
.fi

Contents:

.nf
abcdef12345
.fi

Result:

.nf
API_KEY=abcdef12345
.fi

.SH EMPTY FILES

If a file is empty, the corresponding variable is removed from the environment.

Example:

.nf
env/DEBUG
.fi

(empty file)

Result:

The variable
.B DEBUG
is unset.

.SH EXAMPLES

Run a program with environment variables from a directory:

.nf
envdir ./env ./server
.fi

Directory contents:

.nf
env/DB_HOST  -> localhost
env/DB_PORT  -> 5432
env/DEBUG    -> true
.fi

The executed program receives:

.nf
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DEBUG=true
.fi

.SH EXIT STATUS

If
.B envdir
fails before executing the command, it exits with status
.B 111.

Otherwise, the exit status of the child command is returned.

.SH SEE ALSO

.BR env (1),
.BR daemontools (8)

.SH AUTHOR

Go implementation written as a minimal library and CLI tool inspired by
the original
.B envdir
utility by D. J. Bernstein.

Mat Kovach
